00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.880
- Thank you very much
for the introduction.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.610
So my name is Asanobu Kitamoto

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.120
and Director of ROIS-DS Center

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.250
for Open Data in Humanities.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.520
And professor of National
Institute of Informatics.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.630
So as Haruko introduced,

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:39.900
my original background

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.920
is informatics of computer science,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.140
but I started this humanities
research about 20 years ago

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:52.140
and recently my main focus
research is digital humanities.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:59.400
So I'd like to introduce
today our activities in CODH,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.560
especially in data-driven
approaches for Japan studies.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.560
And so reading Edo we are
working on Edo period data.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.030
So I'd like introduce some of the projects

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.053
that I'm working on in
the Edo period data.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.790
So the ROIS-DS Center for
Open Data in Humanities,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.270
the acronym is CDH.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:31.270
So you can access this URL to
see what the, our activities.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.780
And basically what we are doing
can be categorized into two.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.460
One is data driven humanities,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.540
which is focusing on innovation
in humanities research

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.840
using computer science
technologies and tools.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.730
And another category is
humanities, big data.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:57.580
So it is focusing on innovation
in non humanities research

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.193
using humanities data.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:05.050
So today I will introduce
those two research directions.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:10.650
And so our center is called Open Data.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.380
So open science is the
keyword in our center.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.380
So open data, is on about open science

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:24.100
is to make the research more open.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:29.100
And so it's like we are
collaborating scholars and machines

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:33.100
and citizens collaborate each
other to expand the knowledge

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.420
and increase the knowledge
and deepen the knowledge.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.723
So this is what we are doing in CODH.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.970
And as open data represents,

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.880
we release many types of open data sets

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.000
and this is available in this URL.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.720
So this pre-modern Japanese text,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.543
which is the type books of
pre-modern Japanese books,

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:01.870
which was originally digitized

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.620
by National Institute
of Japanese Literature.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.470
And we host this distribution of data,

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:12.040
from our website and we
create many derivative dataset

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.900
from this classical books
like Edo cooking recipes,

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.667
which is 103 egg dishes with recipes,

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.930
with recipes and photographs

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:31.670
created by professional
cooking researchers.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:37.350
So this is beautiful pictures
and you can reproduce

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.553
the cooking by yourself.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.860
So, and also when we started CODH

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.200
National Institute of Japanese Literate

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:56.500
also started a big project
called Network Project.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.470
And the purpose of this
project is to digitize 300,000

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.130
free modern Japanese books,

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:07.600
which is before 1868 or the Edo period.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.960
And they digitized and
release as open data.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.960
So, the advantage of open data is that

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.833
anyone can use data for any purpose.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.380
Only the requirement
is to give attribution

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:25.620
to National Institute
of Japanese Literature.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.680
So with this project,

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.770
Japanese culture finally
entered into the Big Data Era.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.840
So this is appropriate time

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.993
to start data driven
approaches to Japanese studies.

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:41.970
So I'd like to introduce
some of the projects

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.800
that we have been doing.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.160
And the first one is AI
Kuzushiji Recognition

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.330
from the dataset to the app.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.020
And this project is
collaborator is Tarin Clanuwat

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.990
who was originally a
formally, a CODH researcher,

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.913
and now is working on in Google Brain.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:04.670
So the purpose, so the
motivation of the research

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:08.440
is this digitization of the books.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.390
And if you can see the books,

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:13.250
you can understand the Japanese knowledge

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.420
of a thousand years is recorded

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.260
in the massive amount of books.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:24.260
So these are how to makeup
or how to for 100 cranes

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.000
using one piece of paper,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.840
we call it origami in Japanese
and also how to build automat

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.170
or robots in Edo period.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:37.170
So it's like many kind of
knowledge that existed in the past

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:39.140
is recorded in the books.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:42.550
So we like to take advantage
of these knowledge,

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:47.550
but the main problem, a big problem here

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:53.450
is the characters that is
recording the knowledge.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:55.550
So these are called Kuzushiji

00:07:55.550 --> 00:08:00.550
but unfortunately most
of the Japanese people,

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.820
including me cannot read these kuzushiji.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.750
So even if you digitize
the books and release

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.560
as open data,

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:10.860
most people cannot take
advantage of these knowledge

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:11.693
in the books.

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:17.880
So the problem is that we
have massive documents,

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.280
but very few readers.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:24.030
And some people estimate that
we have one billion documents

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.490
still preserved in Japan before a period.

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:33.350
So we have quite large
number of documents.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:37.540
And, but we have about 10,000 readers

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:41.550
who can read fluently Kuzushiji.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:46.550
So this is quite unbalanced situation.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:50.880
For example, in English, you
have probably many documents,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:51.760
but many readers.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.210
So you can, many people can
read the English characters.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:57.970
So you can, for example,

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:02.970
ask the citizens to read
documents using crowdsourcing

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:04.440
or so on.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.040
But in Japanese Kuzushiji documents,

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.360
we have many, but we have few readers.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.313
So we cannot take advantage
of these documents.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:20.240
So we think that AI or
machine learning is a solution

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:23.163
for solve this problem.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:29.018
So we created Kuzushiji dataset and well,

00:09:29.018 --> 00:09:32.710
this dataset is created
by National Institute

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:35.390
of Japanese Literature
and we created this data

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:37.670
and release it as open data.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:39.500
So as you can see,

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.233
this is the collection
of Kuzushiji character.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.360
This is no, Hiraganano in
the collection of Hiradanano

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.250
And so for each character,

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.540
we collected character from many books

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:59.540
and organize it by characters.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:04.330
And so the open data
consists of character types,

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.760
4,328 character types.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:11.340
And we have more than 1
million character shapes.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.280
So this is a quite large data set.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:21.280
And if you can, if you can
download the deep file,

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:25.083
you can use it as training
data for machine learning.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.490
And so you can develop AI,

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.560
Kuzushiji recognition
using this training data.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:34.020
And so the release of
dataset stimulated research

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:37.283
on AI kuzushiji recognition
and our group is one of them.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:42.490
So you using this dataset, we can develop

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:44.183
AI Kuzushiji recognition,

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.293
but what kind of technology
is useful for this problem?

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:51.827
And we think that the,

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:57.070
the technology called object
detection is useful for this,

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.610
for this to solve this problem.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.410
And this object detection
is developed in the,

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:04.980
in the research field
called computer vision.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:09.590
So it's basically analyzing
the image using algorithms

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.910
and especially object detection
is a vibrant research area

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.560
with industrial value,
such as auto mass driving.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:20.060
So if you can see this image

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:23.830
where object detection algorithm
can detect that this area,

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.460
we have a car and we have a car,

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:28.070
we have a car here and we have bicycle,

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:29.980
we have traffic light and so on.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.320
So you can easily guess
that this is very useful

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.650
for autonomous driving.

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:37.380
So many people are working on
object detection algorithms.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:42.060
And so we have a very good
algorithm for object detection.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.510
So the idea is that, can
we apply this technology

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:46.983
for Kuzushiji characters like this?

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.880
And, well, this is a simple idea.

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.000
This sounds like to be a simple idea,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.720
but it was not possible before.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:58.720
And we tried it and unexpectedly

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:00.473
successful result we get.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.590
So the results we developed
a method called KuroNet

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:12.400
which is a Kuzushiji recognition
using deep learning method.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:15.540
And so if you, well,

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:20.540
you can see that their
Kuzushiji and well the Kuzushiji

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:26.680
you can, these are written
in a connected style.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.680
So where each character is not separated.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.870
But some, a few characters
are sometimes connected.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:36.780
So it is not easy to
divide into one character,

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.767
but using KuroNet you can
detect the character like this.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.803
So this rectangle is called bounding box.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:52.050
So you can see that well even
the character is connected

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:55.000
there it can divide into,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:56.560
well for example, here,

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.810
you can divide it into each character.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:04.810
And then you recognize the
character in the bounding box.

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:09.720
So you can see that these
are Unicode characters

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:13.230
that is used in the modern Japanese.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.890
So starting from Kuzushiji character

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:18.220
you can first detect the bounding box

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:21.530
and then recognize characters.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.364
And so then we can read

00:13:24.364 --> 00:13:27.730
any Japanese can read these characters.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:32.730
So it takes only one or two second for

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:36.880
to recognize all the
characters in one image.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:41.880
So is it's fast enough and
accuracy is sometimes 95%

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.580
for books with the best condition.

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:49.580
So even if you, we have some
several errors in one image,

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:55.400
we can still understand
what is written in one page.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.330
So it is very helpful for
Japanese to understand

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:00.603
what is the content of the books.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:05.800
So this is an example of
AI Kuzushiji recognition.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:10.673
And can I start?

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.240
So when you can see

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.960
the chronic Kuzushiji
recognition service is here,

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:22.250
and this is, I will explain
later that this is called

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:24.300
a IIIF Curation Viewer.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:29.300
And if the books are
distributed or delivered

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:35.520
using IIIF you can use this
viewer and specify the box

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.150
that you want to recognize, Kuzushiji

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:44.070
and you can, after that, you can move to

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.840
Kuzushiji recognition service.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:49.390
And in one, in a few second,

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:53.253
you can see that the characters
are recognized like this.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.360
So you can zoom in or zoom out,

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:00.120
and you can also change the location

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:01.903
or the size of the characters,

00:15:02.757 --> 00:15:07.750
the location of the characters and so on,

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:11.110
so that you can compare
like modern character

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:12.400
with the Kuzushiji character.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:14.080
And then you can, yes.

00:15:15.567 --> 00:15:17.617
And also you can have a clone text editor

00:15:19.499 --> 00:15:23.521
to specify the reading
order of the characters.

00:15:23.521 --> 00:15:27.521
And after that, you can
output the text reading

00:15:30.197 --> 00:15:31.197
of the page.

00:15:36.578 --> 00:15:40.560
Yes, so then you can
copy and paste this text

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:45.560
into editor or so on.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:49.740
So you can take advantage
of this text service.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:53.380
So using this service,

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:57.473
you can first to capture
the image and then,

00:15:59.295 --> 00:16:04.043
and recognize and serialize
and finally you get text.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:06.973
Okay.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:13.170
So we developed KuroNet,
but we want to improve that.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:16.870
We wanted to improve the
machine learning model

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:20.150
to a more higher performance.

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:24.360
So we used Kaggle

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.723
which is the largest AI
competition platform.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:32.890
So we heard a competition
called Kuzushiji Recognition

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:34.020
on Kaggle

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:37.110
And our competition was the
first in the humanities domain.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:41.400
So we released the data set
and ask AI experts in the world

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:47.053
to submit a good model for
AI Kuzushiji Recognition.

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:53.700
So, yeah, we also provided
price money like this.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:57.820
And the period was from
July to October, 2019,

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:02.820
and we had 293 teams with 338 members,

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:08.193
and they submit more
than 2000 submissions.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:14.230
So as a result of this
worldwide competition,

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:20.670
we get a very good model from many people.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:26.430
And the winner model called
Tascj Team looks like this.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:30.620
So if you give Kuzushiji book,

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:33.880
then they can detect the
boundary models like this.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:38.880
And the top accuracy was 95%.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:43.970
So this is quite remarkable because,

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:48.970
well, the characters are mixed
with figures, illustrations,

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:54.280
but they can mostly, they can
detect the only characters,

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:58.050
but not illustrations like this.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.370
So even in a, under the
quite complex layout

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:05.460
they can, the algorithmic
detector Kuzushiji safely

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.203
with a quite high accuracy.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:13.910
And a remarkable point is that all winners

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:15.630
do not read Kuzushiji

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:19.160
but have developed good
machine learning models, so,

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:22.220
or good AI Kuzushiji recognition models.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:27.220
So just focusing on developing
AI Kuzushiji recognition,

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:33.260
they don't need, they need,

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:36.940
they don't have to read
Kuzushiji by human.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:38.560
The machines can read it.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.320
But the import also the
important point is that,

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:42.490
well, the domain knowledge,

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:45.110
we need to have a domain
knowledge about Kuzushiji

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:50.110
what is, how we can create a
dataset for machine learning.

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:52.410
We need a very good domain knowledge.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:54.500
And so, in other words,

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:56.860
the domain knowledge was
embedded in a dataset.

00:18:56.860 --> 00:19:00.750
So the machine AI researchers
do not have to have

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:03.040
the domain knowledge of Kuzushiji.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.880
So it's like open innovation.

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:09.290
We ask people to develop a good AI models,

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:14.290
and then we get this
Al models for wide use.

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:18.950
And after this,

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:22.037
we started developing app
for AI Kuzushiji recognition

00:19:22.037 --> 00:19:24.320
called Miwo.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:26.890
And so we also,

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:31.890
we already released Kuzushiji
recognition service,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:33.910
for IIIF images,

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:38.910
but well sometimes it is more convenient

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:42.630
if you can use your mobile
phone to take a photograph

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:46.810
and then definitely
Kuzushiji in the photograph.

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:49.110
It's more convenient and
especially convenient

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:53.033
if you need to study the material on site,

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.460
move, go somewhere and take a photograph

00:19:56.460 --> 00:20:00.020
and study the document in the old house,

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:02.006
old temple and so on.

00:20:02.006 --> 00:20:05.290
So the app is developed for that purpose.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:09.040
And Miwo the name of Miwo
comes from the 14th chapter

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:11.400
of The Tale of Genji in Miwotsukushi

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:12.930
referring to waterway signs.

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:16.800
So this is Ukiyo-e
showing the Neevo tsukushi

00:20:18.376 --> 00:20:22.393
this shape is Miwotsukushi.

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:25.250
And so just as Miwotsukushi

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.340
is the guide for boats in the sea

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:29.350
we aim to make our Miwo app

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:32.390
as a guide for traveling the
ocean of historical document.

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:34.613
So this is why we name it Miwo.

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:45.380
So this is prototype
app, prototype version,

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:51.600
which is used at the KeMCo
museum in April, 2021.

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:53.030
So you can see that, well,

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:55.250
we have billboard here

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:57.430
and just take a photograph of billboard

00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:00.380
and then Miwo app can recognize characters

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:01.870
on the billboard.

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:06.870
So it doesn't have to
be a flat, flat book.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:10.900
It's like, you can just take a photograph.

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.440
And if it's a little slanted

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:17.860
you can still recognize
characters on the billboard.

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:21.291
So this is, you can see
that this is quite useful

00:21:21.291 --> 00:21:26.291
to recognize Kuzushiji at the site.

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:32.960
And now the Miwo app has this interface.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:34.740
The release version has this interface.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.620
So you can show a recognition
result in characters,

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:40.210
or you can show a recognition result

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:42.500
with bounding boxes like this.

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:47.500
And you can also swipe to
compare the original book

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:49.780
and the recognition result.

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:53.550
And also you can, well,
sometimes AI has errors

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:54.383
so you can,

00:21:54.383 --> 00:21:57.813
you want to modify the
recognition results.

00:21:57.813 --> 00:22:01.600
And we have this interface

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:06.581
and also with reference
to root characters and to.

00:22:06.581 --> 00:22:07.490
(clears throat)

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:12.490
Or also see the data base
of Kuzushiji characters,

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:16.500
and then understand that,
well, probably this is wrong

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:18.760
and another one is correct.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:22.260
And finally, you can also
generate the text output

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:23.420
from the recognition result.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:28.420
So you can copy and paste
to another application

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:30.430
so that you can use it for your research

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.223
or for the purposes.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:36.160
So we summarize the impact

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.210
and future of Kuzushiji recognition.

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:43.210
So the Miwo app was downloaded
more than 42,000 times,

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:47.200
and more than 300,000
images were recognized

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:48.333
since the release.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.070
And the daily uploaded images

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:53.030
is constantly about 2000 images.

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:58.030
So this indicates that
the app is used steadily

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:03.653
and there's demand from the
public to recognize Kuzushiji.

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:09.870
And so we release the app,

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:14.870
but I think the future
is that we want develop

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:18.080
the full-text search engine
of historical documents

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:22.150
like Google, Google, or Google books.

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:26.840
You can search a type the
word and search the books.

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:30.030
And well, we don't have
such full-text search engine

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:32.330
for historical documents of Japanese.

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:36.940
And so we will like to
develop this in a few years.

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:38.910
And we already have the name

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.460
for this full-text search
engine it's called Tsukushi

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:43.360
so it's like Miwo and tsukushi,

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:45.760
so tsukushi part is would be useful

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:47.263
full-text search engine.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:49.530
And I think,

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:51.050
I believe that the full-text search engine

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:53.540
would be the driver of
digital transformation

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:54.980
into humanities research,

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:58.200
especially Japan studies,
because full-text search engine,

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.560
you can see the detail of the books

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:04.260
and then starting from the detail,

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.440
you can expand the search.

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:11.440
So I think that this is
a very important tool

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:15.883
for digital transformation
in humanities research.

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:19.353
Okay.

00:24:19.353 --> 00:24:22.360
So I would like to move
on to the next topic

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.580
about Bukan complete collection.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:27.680
So this collaborator is Kumiko Fujizane

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:29.200
Professor Kumiko Fujizane

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:32.080
in the National Institute
of Japanese Literature.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:36.250
So we talk about character recognition

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:39.990
and well it's after character recognition,

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:42.483
Kuzushiji recognition,
you can analyze a text,

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:47.760
but, and usually digital humanities people

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.140
focus more on text

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:53.770
because the human humanities
research, they're quite

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:01.900
focusing on text interpretation
or text understanding

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:05.550
or text, critical reading, so on.

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:10.320
But in a digital humanities
we need to focus on

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:13.150
also non text materials,

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:15.960
like images and photographs and maps,

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:19.280
and about the character
recognition and so on.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:21.830
So I'd like to emphasize
that this document

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.120
is not only about text.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.080
And so from now on,

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:30.710
we introduced how to
deal with structured data

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:32.700
or unstructured data.

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:35.480
And unstructured data
is include the visual

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:37.990
and special sources like photographs.

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:42.180
It's well, it's a pixel
collection of pixel and the maps.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:47.180
It's also the images or
data, but it's not text.

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:53.770
So they require its own analysis

00:25:53.770 --> 00:25:54.930
interpretation of framework.

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:58.220
So we are also working on
developing this kind of framework

00:25:58.220 --> 00:26:00.473
for non-text sources.

00:26:03.159 --> 00:26:04.460
So the Bukan,

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:07.110
I would like to introduce
the Bukan project, what we,

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:14.950
which is about non-texture
or structured data project.

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:18.330
And this is an example of Bukan book

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:21.030
which is published in 1789,

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:24.480
including in the dataset
of premodern Japanese text.

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.090
So you can see that, well,

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:28.700
this is actually information about Daimyo

00:26:31.423 --> 00:26:32.680
in the other period,

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:37.680
and this is called Crest or
camo is a symbol of a family.

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:41.230
And also this is a name of the Daimyo

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:44.670
and this also includes when they go to Edo

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:48.050
or when they return to their home country,

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:51.530
home state or what they give to Bakufu

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:56.530
or how, what kind of
tools they use for making

00:27:00.586 --> 00:27:03.260
as a symbol of the family or so on.

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:07.900
And also, so these are the
names of important person

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:10.880
in the Daimyo family Daimyo state.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:14.320
So this is a quite a structured data

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:16.150
and the Bukan is a kind of data book

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:18.890
of Daimyo and personnel in the Edo Bakufu

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:23.960
so this has a rich kind of information.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:27.500
An interesting point
is that this Bukan book

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:31.217
has been published for more
than 200 years before 1867

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:34.913
until the end of the Edo period.

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:38.590
So we have actually many versions of Bukan

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:42.343
and also from several publishers.

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:45.930
And also it's a long-seller books.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.750
Actually, it has been
published for 200 years

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:51.343
because it is important and long-seller.

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:55.890
And it has practical usage like it,

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:59.130
some people use it as a
souvenir from Edo City

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:04.110
or some people use it for
seeing the Daimyo gioretz

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:09.110
procession of Daimyo to that these tools

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:14.800
can be used to identify which
family is working so on.

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.600
So, and because of the large demand,

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:19.430
the frequency of updates had increased

00:28:19.430 --> 00:28:21.550
to a few times a month at the peak.

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:25.263
So it has,

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.470
so if you compare a different version,

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:31.610
you can identify that
which part is updated

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:33.830
from the previous version to the next.

00:28:33.830 --> 00:28:37.980
So this kind of frequent
update of the information

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:40.953
is also an interesting
point of Bukan books.

00:28:43.718 --> 00:28:45.447
And from the Bukan books,

00:28:45.447 --> 00:28:50.420
you can create some visualization
or the structured data

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:52.590
like this is a Sankin Kotai animation,

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:57.320
Sankin Kotai is that
Daimyo needs to move from

00:28:57.320 --> 00:29:01.560
their home country to Edo and back,

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.430
they have to move back and forth

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:06.330
because of the order of the Bakufu.

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:09.170
So they, and so it's the animation

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:12.740
of when they move to Edo and
when they leave Edo also.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:16.170
And also this is information
extracted from the Bukan

00:29:16.170 --> 00:29:19.850
that it has some properties of Daimyo

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:24.850
like how many rice crop they can produce

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:29.040
or where they live or so on.

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:31.600
So this kind of structured
data can be created

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:32.823
from Bukan books.

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:36.500
But the problem is that,

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:38.563
well, this is quite heavy work.

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:43.360
And as I said, we have
books over 200 years.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:47.613
So how we can, how can we
transcribe all of them?

00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:53.750
And well, it's not realistic
to transcribe all of them.

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:57.000
So the solution is that, well,

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:58.990
why not detect and transcribe

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:01.660
the difference to create time-series data?

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:03.960
So we call it differential
transcription because,

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:08.283
well if you compare this
book and another book,

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:10.080
you can see that,

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:14.770
well, most of the part looks
like the same, but some part,

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:19.770
for example, this family tree,
this part is absent here.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:25.290
And this character is
also here, but not here.

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:27.940
So there's difference
between the two versions.

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:30.487
So you can probably
identify the difference

00:30:30.487 --> 00:30:33.820
and then transcribe the updated part

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:37.363
to create time-series data.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:43.690
So, well, the idea is
that if you have text,

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:46.240
you can easily compare
the text A and text B

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:47.480
and find difference.

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:48.870
And there are many tools available

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.533
for identifying the text-based difference.

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:55.500
But for using this text-based difference,

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:56.720
we need to transcribe,

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:00.230
or we need to apply OCR
to get text from the image

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:01.820
and then compare.

00:31:01.820 --> 00:31:04.390
But this is not realistic
for the Bukan book,

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:06.670
because some characters difficult to read.

00:31:06.670 --> 00:31:10.670
And also we have graphical elements

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:13.370
which is not character like this.

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:17.200
So the idea is that we
develop an image based

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:18.880
or non-textual difference

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:21.060
by directly comparing the images

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:22.960
and find the difference.

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:24.150
And unfortunately,

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:27.530
there are no tools
available for this purpose.

00:31:27.530 --> 00:31:30.580
And the traditional method
is side by side comparison.

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:35.580
So we put two images side by
side and compare by human eyes.

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:41.053
But is there any better way?

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:44.760
So it's like visual comparison

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:47.360
and it's called find a difference game.

00:31:47.360 --> 00:31:52.123
So you can compare two images
and detect the difference.

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:55.390
But this is quite difficult.

00:31:55.390 --> 00:32:00.390
And well, actually it is
difficult so it is a game so.

00:32:02.195 --> 00:32:04.278
(coughs)

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:08.700
Not easy, but if you use,
if you can take advantage

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:13.280
of the computer, computer
can overlay to images

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:15.420
and emphasize the difference,

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:17.530
and then you can easily identify that

00:32:17.530 --> 00:32:19.390
well, this part is different,
this part is different.

00:32:19.390 --> 00:32:22.420
This part, this part,
those part are different.

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:27.420
So it's not difficult.

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:31.903
So it's not a game, it's
the easy game for computers.

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:35.440
So the important point is that for humans,

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.330
visual comparison requires an effort

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.063
comparable to playing games.

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:42.730
Is not it's difficult, but for machines,

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:45.170
visual comparison is an easy game

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:48.260
using computer vision based
image, matching algorithm.

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:53.260
So, let's turn a difficult
task reading difference

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:56.580
into an easy one with
the help of machines.

00:32:56.580 --> 00:33:00.840
So if we provide a
humans with this figure,

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:02.120
humans can easily identify

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:03.770
that what this part is different.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:09.070
So instead of showing these two images,

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:13.300
we create this image
and show it to humans,

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:15.300
and then they can identify the difference.

00:33:15.300 --> 00:33:17.820
So we call it differential reading.

00:33:17.820 --> 00:33:19.390
So it's a new mode of reading books

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:22.040
focusing on difference
between additions or versions.

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:26.770
And we released a service
called image correlation

00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:30.020
for differentiate reading on this URL.

00:33:30.020 --> 00:33:32.494
So it, we develop a JavaScript-based tool

00:33:32.494 --> 00:33:37.494
vdiff.js for comparing images
and for on this website, you,

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:41.140
anyone can upload two
images or specify URLs,

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:43.180
and the system can
automatically match two images

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:44.463
and emphasize difference.

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:46.880
And when a system fails,

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:49.160
you can manually improve the matching.

00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:54.160
So, and this Genji
Hyakunin Isshu comparison,

00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:56.060
this is not released by us,

00:33:56.060 --> 00:33:59.770
but it's released from the
University of Tokyo Library.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.860
And they compare Genji Hyakunin Isshu

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:05.360
images from two different libraries

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:10.360
and compare them on the
vdiff.js that we provide.

00:34:10.691 --> 00:34:15.460
And they notice that there's
difference on this part,

00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:18.840
but characters are the same,
but only the facial part is,

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:20.350
has difference.

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:22.830
So we don't know why they are different,

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:24.470
but it is an interesting observation.

00:34:24.470 --> 00:34:27.880
And it is not easy to notice by humans

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:29.630
by just comparing side by side,

00:34:29.630 --> 00:34:33.120
but overlaying two images,
you can easily identify that

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:35.040
well, there is actually difference.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:36.910
So why there's difference?

00:34:36.910 --> 00:34:41.670
It is a new research
question that to ask for

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:46.623
and study more about how
books are created and so on.

00:34:49.715 --> 00:34:54.610
And this image collection
service is for two images,

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:56.334
but for the Bukan

00:34:56.334 --> 00:34:59.253
can we want to compare
books or book collection.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:03.840
So we develop a system for book collection

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:05.763
and a large scale book collection.

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:10.960
So I like briefly
introduce how we do this.

00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:13.870
And it's consists of page
collation, book collation

00:35:13.870 --> 00:35:15.020
and woodblock tracking.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:20.340
The first part is page collation.

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:23.440
So we compare two images
and overlay two images

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:25.499
using keypoint matching.

00:35:25.499 --> 00:35:28.190
and keypoint I don't go into
the detail of the algorithm,

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:31.720
but basically we detect
the key points like this,

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:35.600
which is kind of has a
unique intensity change

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:40.050
in a small region and
then compared to images

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:45.050
that has similar keypoints
and then make a correspondence

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:48.370
between keypoints and then compare to how

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:52.240
we can overlay two images
with minimum error.

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:55.543
So this using this algorithm,
you can compare two images.

00:35:57.950 --> 00:35:59.830
And also in a book level,

00:35:59.830 --> 00:36:04.420
we compare book each page from book A

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:05.640
and each page from book B

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:09.990
and see that how many key
points we can make a matching.

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:13.430
And then we can order by score

00:36:13.430 --> 00:36:16.420
and apply the stable marriage algorithm

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:18.090
to find the best match.

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:21.970
So stable marriage algorithm
basically have a preference.

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:26.370
It is originally developed
for the optimal marriage

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:28.500
between the men and women.

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:32.150
And the requirement is
that each man and women

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:35.113
has a preference order that this,

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:40.537
this lady A has preference
on 3524 men and so on.

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:46.530
So if you define this preference order,

00:36:46.530 --> 00:36:51.280
then we can find the best
matching miss marriage.

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:54.880
So we apply this algorithms
for making best match

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:56.323
between book A and book B.

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:03.230
So this is an example of
a page by page collation.

00:37:03.230 --> 00:37:06.960
So you can see that what this part is

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:11.140
red is from the, this book publish in 1791

00:37:11.140 --> 00:37:15.400
and the blue part is from the 1789.

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:17.560
So there are two years apart.

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:22.560
And you can see that some
parts are missing in book B

00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:26.930
and blue book, and some parts
are missing in red book,

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:29.040
and you can easily identify

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:34.040
that this which part is
updated in two versions.

00:37:38.820 --> 00:37:43.060
Also, you can have one image
showing the difference easily

00:37:43.910 --> 00:37:47.850
by using red and blue color.

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:49.580
And if the matching is not good,

00:37:49.580 --> 00:37:52.270
then you can change in real time

00:37:52.270 --> 00:37:56.143
the how we improve the
matching between two images.

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:02.340
So after this,

00:38:02.340 --> 00:38:06.840
we can keep tracking of
the woodblock across time.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:09.653
So for example, this book
is published in 1889,

00:38:10.950 --> 00:38:12.920
and this book is published in 8041.

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:17.860
So it's their difference,

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:19.760
but you can overlay two images

00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:21.900
and then compare the difference.

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:24.050
So the same woodblock
can be tracked to analyze

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:26.090
evolution of information on the woodblock

00:38:26.090 --> 00:38:30.080
and this is useful for
differential transcription

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:32.930
to identify the difference
and then transcribe that part.

00:38:36.170 --> 00:38:38.600
Okay so we move to the third part,

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:41.190
Kaokore and IIIF Curation Platform.

00:38:41.190 --> 00:38:43.480
And this collaborator is Chikahiko Suzuki

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:46.873
and Jun Homma and Yingtao
Tian from Google Brain.

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:52.260
So I'd like to introduce the IIIF

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:57.260
which is maybe some of you
have already known the IIIF

00:38:57.330 --> 00:38:59.830
but this IIIF is called

00:38:59.830 --> 00:39:02.350
International Image
Interoperability Framework

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:07.350
and well to understand easily,

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:13.170
I sometimes say that on
the web, you have HTML.

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:16.523
And if you create HTML page,

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:22.260
you can have many trip
web service, one to three,

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:26.570
and you can, if you use the web browser,

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:29.800
you can see all the web service

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:32.400
using one viewer, one browser,

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:37.400
and you have several
browsers like Chrome and Edge

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:44.310
and Firefox and so on, but
using different browser,

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:49.310
you can use this web service everywhere

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:51.600
using the same browser.

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:55.817
So IIIF feeds HTML four images.

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:01.670
So you have many IIIF service in the word,

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:04.970
and you have few IIIF viewer,

00:40:04.970 --> 00:40:08.150
but if you've have used one IIIF viewer,

00:40:08.150 --> 00:40:12.483
then you can see any IIIF
service using one viewer.

00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:18.660
So I introduced some of
the information about IIIF

00:40:19.800 --> 00:40:23.120
in the seminar but this is, yes,

00:40:23.120 --> 00:40:25.680
you can understand that this is images,

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:29.293
IIIF is like web, HTML in the web.

00:40:31.010 --> 00:40:35.123
And we are interested in
using IIIF for curation.

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:38.950
And curation is a word
that originally means

00:40:38.950 --> 00:40:41.120
activities at museums such
as collecting materials

00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:42.623
and exhibiting artworks.

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:45.923
So we, if we have,

00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:49.300
so it's like a process
of collecting materials

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:52.910
under a theme and arrange
them in an appropriate order

00:40:52.910 --> 00:40:57.100
or layout and present or share
the result as a new material.

00:40:57.100 --> 00:40:59.343
So this is a step of curation.

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:03.960
And if we want to create a curation,

00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:08.257
like the collection of
faces in analog age,

00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:14.600
we need to take a photograph
or copy the material

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:18.630
and using scissors to crop the part

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:23.630
and use the glue to collect
them as a new collection.

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:28.103
So it is a very time consuming
work in analog world.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:31.710
But in a digital world,

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:35.330
you can create a collection quite easily

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:37.020
using IIIF curation viewer,

00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:40.270
which is developed by COD since 2016.

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:43.220
So you can read a IIIF image,

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:47.620
and you can first use this
rectangle button crop button

00:41:47.620 --> 00:41:49.630
to select the region,

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:52.440
and then use the star favorite button

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:56.003
to input into curation
to correct into curation.

00:41:57.160 --> 00:42:02.160
So if you read new IIIF
images and crop the region,

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:06.480
and then add to curation,
and by doing this,

00:42:06.480 --> 00:42:09.200
you can create your own
collection of images

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:11.143
from the other part of image.

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:15.540
So using this approach,

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:20.540
we created a collection of
facial expressions or KaoKore

00:42:21.705 --> 00:42:24.616
Kao is a face and Kore is a collection so.

00:42:24.616 --> 00:42:26.320
(coughs)

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:30.430
With this as a new dataset
for facial expressions.

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:32.630
So using IIIF viewer for cropping

00:42:32.630 --> 00:42:34.500
and collecting apart images,

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:37.320
and we use another tool
IIIF Curation Finder

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:39.300
for searching the collection by metadata.

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:44.300
Like this is image of warriors,

00:42:45.910 --> 00:42:50.420
which is added as a metadata
of these books, these images.

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:55.060
And finally we use IIIF curation
board finalizing collection

00:42:55.060 --> 00:43:00.033
for art history research in the
digital humanities research.

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:06.240
So this IIIF curation board, you can,

00:43:08.735 --> 00:43:13.279
well first you create a
user IIIF creation viewer

00:43:13.279 --> 00:43:16.788
to crop the regions and use
create KaoKore collection,

00:43:16.788 --> 00:43:21.038
and then use IIIF creation
board to organize images

00:43:22.260 --> 00:43:26.920
on two dimensional board.

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:30.287
And you can place for
example, this sheet mat,

00:43:33.790 --> 00:43:38.130
or to make a grouping or add a stamp

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:43.130
to add another more smaller
groupings of the images

00:43:44.937 --> 00:43:46.490
and so on.

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:50.090
So you can move these images
to another group or so on,

00:43:50.090 --> 00:43:53.670
so that these are actually the criteria

00:43:53.670 --> 00:43:55.570
of using art history research

00:43:55.570 --> 00:43:58.150
that how to study this book.

00:43:58.150 --> 00:44:02.330
And you can, because this is IIIF you can,

00:44:02.330 --> 00:44:05.960
anytime go back to the
original image to understand

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:08.523
in which context this phase appears.

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:12.210
So it's like, you can go,

00:44:12.210 --> 00:44:15.390
you can first study the detail of the face

00:44:15.390 --> 00:44:17.053
and then return to the,

00:44:18.810 --> 00:44:23.640
or the macro scale analysis
is including the context

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:24.633
of the face.

00:44:28.340 --> 00:44:31.730
And we also developed a face
detection by machine learning,

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:35.440
which is similar to the
Kuzushiji character.

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:39.480
So the same technology can
be applied to face detection

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:41.090
in the artwork.

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:44.861
And these are algorithm successfully

00:44:44.861 --> 00:44:48.400
detected the faces in artworks,

00:44:48.400 --> 00:44:50.050
learned from the KaoKore dataset.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:56.250
So this is like ML a machine
learning assisted Annotation.

00:44:56.250 --> 00:44:58.770
So it's like learning
from the KaoKore dataset

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:01.850
about 80% of the faces
automatically detected.

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:06.850
And we apply this
technology to new artworks

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:11.850
is still detected 70% of the faces.

00:45:11.850 --> 00:45:15.300
So it means that two thirds of the faces

00:45:15.300 --> 00:45:17.380
can be automatically detected.

00:45:17.380 --> 00:45:20.303
So the amount of work by
humans is reduced to one third.

00:45:22.430 --> 00:45:24.790
Then art historians can analyze more data

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:26.410
and more data leads to richer evidence

00:45:26.410 --> 00:45:28.540
and higher reliability of the results.

00:45:28.540 --> 00:45:33.540
So it's like using machine
learning and create a new dataset

00:45:33.830 --> 00:45:38.830
which is useful for
expanding the dataset sites

00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:44.543
and useful for art history research.

00:45:46.360 --> 00:45:49.420
And another technology for face detection

00:45:49.420 --> 00:45:52.320
is used to create Ukiyo-e faces dataset,

00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:56.890
which is created by Yingtao
Tian from Google Brain.

00:45:56.890 --> 00:45:58.950
And well, the original image

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:01.520
is from Art Research Center
Ritsemeikan University

00:46:01.520 --> 00:46:05.740
and well it's Ukiyo-e and Kabuki player,

00:46:08.512 --> 00:46:12.954
mostly Kabuki player and Kabuki
player faces are extracted

