we can specify the place name

00:51:38.010 --> 00:51:41.993
across the different applications.

00:51:42.870 --> 00:51:47.870
And For example, we created
the Edo Sightseeing guide.

00:51:49.670 --> 00:51:52.570
We selected two tribal
guide books for each century

00:51:52.570 --> 00:51:55.570
and used IIIF Curation Viewer
to crop pictorial parts

00:51:55.570 --> 00:51:58.250
and add metadata by transcription.

00:51:58.250 --> 00:52:01.070
So, and assign GeoLOD
and other identifiers.

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:02.870
So this is image of Asakusa and yes,

00:52:08.103 --> 00:52:11.210
we give an identifier,
a jewelry identifier

00:52:11.210 --> 00:52:15.600
to this image so we can use this image

00:52:15.600 --> 00:52:18.923
in another application to
show the image of Asakusa.

00:52:21.350 --> 00:52:23.360
We also created Edo shopping guide,

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:27.410
and yes it's like the database

00:52:27.410 --> 00:52:31.420
of shopping guidebook published in 1824.

00:52:31.420 --> 00:52:34.630
And yeah, we classified the
type of merchants business

00:52:34.630 --> 00:52:37.380
according to this system and assigned Edo

00:52:37.380 --> 00:52:38.870
the and identify it.

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.550
So we can know that this
is a place of the shops.

00:52:42.550 --> 00:52:46.673
And then we know that which area we have

00:52:46.673 --> 00:52:51.673
this kind of food,
wholesale shops and so on.

00:52:53.780 --> 00:52:58.280
And now those information is
integrated into edomi data port

00:52:58.280 --> 00:52:59.920
for the historical Edo.

00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:03.730
And so we can visualize
that where the distribution

00:53:03.730 --> 00:53:08.423
of the shops and sight seeing
spots on the current map.

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:14.890
So it's like looking back the history.

00:53:14.890 --> 00:53:19.240
So we, well, for the
example shopping guide,

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:24.170
we created the type,
classification of the business

00:53:24.170 --> 00:53:27.913
using the current Japan standard
industrial classification.

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:30.900
So then we know that, well,

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:34.250
we have many medicine shops
and household hardware

00:53:34.250 --> 00:53:35.230
and so on.

00:53:35.230 --> 00:53:39.370
So we can easily understand
that how the business

00:53:39.370 --> 00:53:41.963
distribution goes in Edo period.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:47.350
And the reason I'm doing this is that,

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:50.300
well, we believe that the general public

00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:52.610
wants to look back the
history from the present.

00:53:52.610 --> 00:53:54.520
So it is easier to understand

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:57.610
using the current classification scheme.

00:53:57.610 --> 00:54:00.950
And what we know that experts
try to understand the past

00:54:00.950 --> 00:54:01.920
as it was.

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:06.043
So they try to study the
material that it was,

00:54:07.011 --> 00:54:12.011
but we will write link
the past from the present

00:54:12.740 --> 00:54:16.500
to understand for the general public

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:18.113
to understand more easily.

00:54:21.310 --> 00:54:26.100
So similar idea is actually
explored in the Europe

00:54:26.100 --> 00:54:28.020
for time machine Europe project,

00:54:28.020 --> 00:54:30.430
and they call it big data of the past.

00:54:30.430 --> 00:54:33.140
So they create a machine
readable data of the past

00:54:33.140 --> 00:54:34.800
using AI and simulation

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:37.140
and developing new critical reflections

00:54:37.140 --> 00:54:38.840
of past and the future.

00:54:38.840 --> 00:54:43.840
So it's like we want to
create a big data of the past

00:54:44.370 --> 00:54:47.370
and then connect it with the present

00:54:47.370 --> 00:54:49.330
and then extend it to the future.

00:54:49.330 --> 00:54:52.700
And I think that is the
very important direction

00:54:52.700 --> 00:54:54.023
of the future research.

00:54:54.970 --> 00:54:57.320
So we are working on
historical big data project.

00:54:57.320 --> 00:55:01.700
It's like creating a structured
data from historical sources

00:55:01.700 --> 00:55:05.510
on the nature data and culture
data, including weather,

00:55:05.510 --> 00:55:07.153
earthquake, eruption, disease,

00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:10.760
economy, population politic, and culture.

00:55:10.760 --> 00:55:11.873
And this is very,

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:18.270
extracting information of the past

00:55:18.270 --> 00:55:20.870
and use it for the present and the future.

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:25.040
So now what the key point is
data structuring workflow,

00:55:25.040 --> 00:55:28.300
starting from handwritten
characters to digital image

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:32.090
and plain text and by
transcription and by markup

00:55:32.090 --> 00:55:34.270
to semi-structured data.

00:55:34.270 --> 00:55:38.820
But then we also want to
analyze with the current word,

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:42.240
like latitude and longitude.

00:55:42.240 --> 00:55:46.800
So we like to make mapping
between the document space

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:47.960
and entity space.

00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:50.270
I don't have time to explain the details.

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:52.100
So just to show the figure,

00:55:52.100 --> 00:55:56.000
but the important point is
that we like to create link,

00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:58.703
make a link between the
document and the word.

00:56:00.290 --> 00:56:03.340
So this is the summary of today's talk.

00:56:03.340 --> 00:56:06.850
We introduce the data-driven
approaches for Japan studies.

00:56:06.850 --> 00:56:09.230
So the first project is
AI kuzushiji recognition.

00:56:09.230 --> 00:56:11.430
It's illustrates how
machine learning project

00:56:11.430 --> 00:56:15.410
can be started and developed
into the real word application

00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:18.530
And Bukan complete production shows

00:56:18.530 --> 00:56:20.100
how the idea of differential reading

00:56:20.100 --> 00:56:22.053
can reduce the burden of humans.

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:27.150
And third one KaoKore demonstrates
that how interoperability

00:56:27.150 --> 00:56:29.270
such as IIIF plays a critical role

00:56:29.270 --> 00:56:31.820
in digital humanities platform.

00:56:31.820 --> 00:56:33.330
And finally the Edo maps

00:56:33.330 --> 00:56:35.240
and historical big data explores

00:56:35.240 --> 00:56:36.850
new possibilities for linking the past,

00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:37.950
present in the future.

